    "problem_statement": "Connection EOF handling broken when reader has read_eof method\n\nWhen a connection has a reader with a `read_eof` method, the EOF handling logic is inverted, causing `ConnectionClosed()` to be returned instead of calling the reader's `read_eof()` method.\n\n#### Steps to Reproduce\n\n```python\nimport h11\n\n# Create a connection and simulate EOF with a reader that has read_eof method\nc = h11.Connection(h11.CLIENT)\nc.send(h11.Request(method='GET', target='/', headers=[('Host', 'example.com')]))\nc.send(h11.EndOfMessage())\n\n# Receive HTTP/1.0 response (which uses connection close for framing)\nc.receive_data(b'HTTP/1.0 200 OK\\r\\n\\r\\n12345')\n\n# Simulate EOF\nc.receive_data(b'')\nevent = c.next_event()\nprint(f\"Got event: {event}\")\n# Should get EndOfMessage, but gets ConnectionClosed instead\n```\n\nThe issue occurs when the connection's reader has a `read_eof` method - the conditional logic is backwards, so it returns `ConnectionClosed()` when it should call `read_eof()`, and vice versa.\n\nThis affects HTTP/1.0 connections and other scenarios where EOF is used to signal the end of message body data."
